  "generated_text": "To determine the length of the belt that is drawn tightly around three circles of radius \\(10\\) cm each, we need to analyze the shape and the geometry of the belt.\n\n1. **Identify the Configuration:**\n   The belt is drawn tightly around three circles, forming a shape that resembles a \"Y\" with a central part connecting the circles. Each circle has a radius of \\(10\\) cm.\n\n2. **Calculate the Distance Between the Centers of the Circles:**\n   Since the circles are identical and the belt is drawn tightly around them, the centers of the circles form an equilateral triangle with each side equal to the diameter of one circle. The diameter of each circle is \\(2 \\times 10 = 20\\) cm. Therefore, the distance between the centers of any two circles is \\(20\\) cm.\n\n3. **Determine the Length of the Belt:**\n   The belt consists of three straight segments (each connecting two centers) and three circular arcs (each segment of the belt that follows the circumference of one circle).\n\n   - **Length of the Straight Segments:**\n     There are three straight segments, each of length \\(20\\) cm. Thus, the total length of the straight segments is:\n     \\[\n     3 \\times 20 = 60 \\text{ cm}\n     \\]\n\n   - **Length of the Circular Arcs:**\n     Each arc is a quarter-circle (since the belt follows the circumference of one circle and there are three such arcs). The circumference of one circle is:\n     \\[\n     2\\pi \\times 10 = 20\\pi \\text{ cm}\n     \\]\n     Each quarter-circle arc has a length of:\n     \\[\n     \\frac{1}{4} \\times 20\\pi = 5\\pi \\text{ cm}\n     \\]\n     There are three such arcs, so the total length of the circular arcs is:\n     \\[\n     3 \\times 5\\pi = 15\\pi \\text{ cm}\n     \\]\n\n4. **Sum the Lengths:**\n   The total length of the belt is the sum of the lengths of the straight segments and the circular arcs:\n   \\[\n   60 + 15\\pi \\text{ cm}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Identify \\(a\\) and \\(b\\):**\n   From the expression \\(60 + 15\\pi\\), we identify \\(a = 60\\) and \\(b = 15\\).\n\n6. **Calculate \\(a + b\\):**\n   \\[\n   a + b = 60 + 15 = 75\n   \\]\n\nThus, the value of \\(a + b\\) is \\(\\boxed{75}\\).",
